  dialogue: [
    { speaker: 'Host',   text: 'Welcome to WKU Radio. Today we\'re asking students to describe a place that matters to them. First, we have Daniyar. Daniyar, tell us about your place.',    isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Daniyar',text: 'My place is a small café in Aktobe — the city where I grew up. It\'s a place that I\'ve been going to since I was about twelve years old.',               isLeft: true  },
    { speaker: 'Host',   text: 'Can you describe it for us?',                                                                                                                                isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Daniyar',text: 'It\'s a café which my grandmother used to take me to every Saturday. It\'s a small, slightly old-fashioned place that serves traditional Kazakh food. The walls are covered in old photographs which show Aktobe in the 1970s and 80s.',  isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Host',   text: 'What makes it special for you?',                                                                                                                             isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Daniyar',text: 'It\'s the people who run it. An elderly couple who have worked there for decades. They know everyone by name. It\'s a place where you always feel recognised — like you matter to someone.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Host',   text: 'Has it changed much since you were young?',                                                                                                                  isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Daniyar',text: 'Very little. The chairs are the same ones that were there when I was a child. The menu, which hasn\'t changed since the 1990s, still has the same soup that my grandmother always ordered.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Host',   text: 'Thank you. Next is Kamila. Kamila, what\'s your place?',                                                                                                    isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Kamila', text: 'Mine is actually a library — not WKU, but the public library in Almaty where I used to go as a teenager. It\'s a large, beautiful building that was built in the Soviet era.',  isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Host',   text: 'What do you remember most about it?',                                                                                                                       isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Kamila', text: 'The silence. And the smell. And the feeling that you were surrounded by ideas that someone had cared about enough to write down. It\'s a place that made me want to become the kind of person who also cared about ideas.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Host',   text: 'Beautiful. Do you still go there?',                                                                                                                         isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Kamila', text: 'Every time I\'m in Almaty. I sit in the same reading room where I used to study for exams. It\'s become a place that connects me to who I was at sixteen.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Host',   text: 'Thank you both. You\'ve described places that clearly define who you are.',                                                                                  isLeft: false },
  ],

