  dialogue: [
    { speaker: 'Moderator', text: 'Welcome to the WKU Student Debate Club. Today\'s motion: "University education should be free for all students." Speaking for the motion: Kamila. Against: Amir. Kamila, you have the floor.',  isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Kamila',    text: 'Thank you. I believe university education should be free, and I\'ll give you three reasons. Firstly, education is a public good. When citizens are educated, the whole society benefits. Therefore, it should be funded publicly. Furthermore, student debt is a significant burden that disproportionately affects students from low-income families. Although wealthier students can absorb this cost, poorer students often cannot. As a result, the current system reinforces inequality rather than reducing it.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Moderator', text: 'Thank you, Kamila. Amir?',                                                                                                                                                                          isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Amir',      text: 'Kamila makes some compelling points. Nevertheless, I believe free university education is not the most effective use of public funds. Firstly, university education primarily benefits the individual — graduates earn more, on average, than non-graduates. Therefore, it is reasonable to expect them to contribute to the cost. Moreover, free tuition might reduce educational quality. If universities are fully funded by the state, they have less incentive to compete for students by improving their programmes.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Kamila',    text: 'Despite the individual benefits, higher earnings also mean higher tax contributions over a lifetime. Consequently, the state does receive a return on its investment. Furthermore, the argument that free universities lose quality ignores the evidence from countries like Germany and Norway, where university education is free and quality is high.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Amir',      text: 'Even though Germany and Norway have free university systems, these are small, wealthy countries with different economic structures. Although their models work there, they may not be transferable to Kazakhstan without significant tax increases that could harm economic growth. Nevertheless, I agree that access to education should be improved. However, targeted scholarships and grants for low-income students are a more cost-effective solution.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Kamila',    text: 'While targeted scholarships are useful, they are insufficient. As a result, many students from middle-income families who don\'t qualify for scholarships still struggle with debt. In addition, means-testing creates bureaucracy and stigma. Therefore, universal free education is a simpler and more equitable solution.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Moderator', text: 'Thank you both. This has been an excellent example of structured academic debate. We\'ll now open the floor to questions.',                                                                          isLeft: false },
  ],

