  title: 'The Story That Wasn\'t Finished: Two Writers Talk',
  type: 'listening', xp: 80, duration: 50,
  paragraphs: [],
  dialogue: [
    { speaker: 'Leila', text: 'Have you finished your narrative writing assignment? I\'ve been struggling with it all week.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Marcus', text: 'Not yet. I wrote three different openings and deleted them all. The problem is I keep starting with too much background information.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Leila', text: 'Same! I spent two pages describing the setting before anything even happened. My teacher said the reader was "waiting for the story to start."', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Marcus', text: 'That\'s exactly the feedback I got too. She said: "Jump in. The reader doesn\'t need to know everything before the first action."', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Leila', text: 'So what did you do? Did you rewrite it?', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Marcus', text: 'I started in the middle of the action — in medias res, you know? The opening line is now: "The door was already open when she arrived." That sets up the past continuous and the main action at the same time.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Leila', text: 'Oh, I like that. It immediately creates a question: why was the door open? The reader wants to know.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Marcus', text: 'Exactly. And then I used the past perfect to fill in the backstory — what had happened before she got there. But only in small pieces, not all at once.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Leila', text: 'That\'s what I\'ve been trying to do. But my problem is tense consistency. I keep slipping into the present tense by accident.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Marcus', text: 'I had that problem too. My teacher told me to read each paragraph aloud. If something sounds wrong, it usually is. Our ear knows the language better than our eye sometimes.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Leila', text: 'That\'s a good tip. What about dialogue? I find it hard to integrate conversations without losing the narrative flow.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Marcus', text: 'I keep dialogue short and purposeful. Each line should reveal character or move the plot. If it doesn\'t do one of those, it shouldn\'t be there.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Leila', text: 'That\'s strict! But probably right. What\'s your story actually about?', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Marcus', text: 'It\'s about a woman who returns to her childhood home after twenty years. She\'s looking for something — but what she finds is not what she expected.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Leila', text: 'That sounds really interesting. The classic return narrative — but with a twist. I\'d read that.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Marcus', text: 'I hope it works. The hardest part is the ending. I don\'t know whether to leave it open or resolve it clearly.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Leila', text: 'I think that depends on what the story is about at its core. If it\'s about ambiguity and uncertainty, leave it open. If it\'s about finding something, close it.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Marcus', text: 'You know, that\'s actually really helpful. I think it\'s about uncertainty. So maybe the door at the end should be open again.', isLeft: false },
  ],
