  paragraphs: [],
  dialogue: [
    { speaker: 'Nadia', text: 'I read something interesting this morning — apparently people in developed countries now spend an average of seven hours a day looking at screens. Seven hours! That\'s nearly half our waking lives.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Kiran', text: 'Is that supposed to be shocking? I mean, a lot of that is work — video calls, documents, research. It\'s not all social media.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Nadia', text: 'Fair point. But there\'s research suggesting that heavy social media use is linked to anxiety and depression, especially in young people. I find myself feeling worse after scrolling for a while.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Kiran', text: 'That\'s real for a lot of people. But it\'s not the technology itself — it\'s how we use it. I use my phone to connect with my family in another country. Without it, I genuinely wouldn\'t feel close to them.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Nadia', text: 'That\'s a good example. Technology can genuinely bring people together across distance. But I wonder if it\'s also pulling people apart when they\'re in the same room.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Kiran', text: 'You mean the "phubbing" problem — people ignoring each other in favour of their phones? Yes, that\'s real too. It\'s a balance issue. The technology isn\'t going anywhere, so we need to be more intentional about it.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Nadia', text: 'What worries me more is the AI job thing. My sister is a paralegal and she\'s convinced her job will be automated within five years. She\'s starting to retrain now, but she doesn\'t know what skills will still be relevant by the time she finishes.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Kiran', text: 'That uncertainty is real. But I think there\'s a difference between "AI will take my job" and "AI will change my job." Most paralegals in ten years will probably be working with AI tools rather than being replaced by them.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Nadia', text: 'I hope you\'re right. But the people who struggle are going to be those who can\'t afford retraining, or who are too old to start again. The disruption isn\'t going to fall equally.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Kiran', text: 'Absolutely. Which is why government support matters — retraining funds, social safety nets. The technology will keep advancing whether we like it or not. The question is whether our social institutions will keep up.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Nadia', text: 'That\'s the thing. Technology moves fast. Policy moves slow. By the time regulation catches up, the damage is often done.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Kiran', text: 'True. But it\'s not hopeless. There are actually some good models of tech governance out there — the EU\'s approach to data privacy, for instance. The GDPR has changed how companies handle personal data globally.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Nadia', text: 'That\'s a fair example. So perhaps the answer is: technology will keep changing our lives, for better and worse, and the work is in shaping how it does that.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Kiran', text: 'Exactly. Optimism doesn\'t mean ignoring the problems. It means believing we can solve them — if we choose to.', isLeft: false },
  ],
