  paragraphs: [],
  dialogue: [
    { speaker: 'Aisha', text: 'So you\'ve just had a paper rejected by three journals in a row. How are you feeling?', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Takeshi', text: 'Honestly? A strange mixture of disappointed and weirdly optimistic. The reviewers\' feedback was actually more useful than I expected. Two of them identified a methodological gap I hadn\'t fully considered.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Aisha', text: 'That\'s the ideal outcome from peer review, isn\'t it? Not just a gatekeeping function but actual intellectual engagement.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Takeshi', text: 'Exactly. The third reviewer was less constructive — essentially repeated our arguments back to us and said they found them unconvincing without explaining why. But two out of three being genuinely helpful is better than average.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Aisha', text: 'What was the methodological gap they identified?', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Takeshi', text: 'We hadn\'t sufficiently controlled for the participants\' prior experience with the task. It turns out that people with even minimal exposure perform considerably better than those with none. So our effect sizes were probably overestimated.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Aisha', text: 'That\'s significant. That\'s exactly the kind of confounding variable that can make a result look more robust than it is.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Takeshi', text: 'Yes. So we\'re redesigning the study with a pre-screening questionnaire to control for that. The sample will be slightly smaller as a result, but the results will be much more interpretable.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Aisha', text: 'Are you worried about statistical power? A smaller sample tends to produce less reliable estimates.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Takeshi', text: 'That\'s the tension, yes. We\'re running simulations to find the minimum sample size that still gives us adequate power. The preliminary analysis suggests around 80 participants should be sufficient.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Aisha', text: 'Has this changed how you think about the original findings? Do you still believe the core hypothesis?', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Takeshi', text: 'I believe there\'s a real effect there. The question is how large it is. Our original estimate may have been inflated. But I\'d be surprised if it vanished completely once we control properly. The effect appears to be moderate at worst.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Aisha', text: 'That\'s actually a mature scientific attitude. A lot of people, once they\'ve publicly committed to a hypothesis, find it very hard to acknowledge when the effect size is smaller than they hoped.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Takeshi', text: 'I know. And that\'s exactly where replication matters — not just for verification, but for calibration. You need multiple studies to get a reliable estimate of the true effect size. Any single study, however well-designed, is just one data point.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Aisha', text: 'The history of science is full of effects that seemed enormous in early studies and became much smaller once the evidence base grew.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Takeshi', text: 'The "winner\'s curse" — inflated effect sizes in early, small studies. The more studies are done on something, the more the estimate tends to converge on the true value, which is usually more modest.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Aisha', text: 'I love how science gradually gets more honest with itself. It\'s frustrating in the short term, but it\'s the whole point.', isLeft: true },
  ],
