const L78_2: FullLesson = {
  id: 'l78-2', moduleNum: 20, lessonNum: 2,
  moduleTitle: 'Law, Rights & Justice',
  title: 'Human Rights: Universal Principles and Contested Realities',
  type: 'reading', xp: 80, duration: 55,
  paragraphs: [
    'Human rights are rights that are held by all people, everywhere, simply by virtue of being human. They are not granted by governments and cannot be legitimately taken away by them. They are universal — applying regardless of nationality, race, gender, religion, or legal status — and they are inalienable, meaning they cannot be permanently surrendered or transferred. This is the theory. The practice is considerably more complicated.',
    'The idea of universal human rights has ancient roots — in Stoic philosophy, in religious traditions that hold all human beings to be of equal dignity, and in Enlightenment natural rights thinking. But the contemporary international human rights regime was constructed largely in the aftermath of the Second World War, as a direct response to the Holocaust and the other atrocities of the war. The Universal Declaration of Human Rights, adopted by the United Nations General Assembly on 10 December 1948, was the founding document of this regime. It articulated thirty articles covering civil, political, economic, social, and cultural rights — from the right not to be tortured to the right to education.',
    'Civil and political rights — freedom of speech, the right to a fair trial, protection from torture, the right to vote — are sometimes called "first generation" rights. Economic, social, and cultural rights — the right to work, to education, to healthcare, to an adequate standard of living — are sometimes called "second generation" rights. A third category, "third generation" rights, covers collective rights such as the right to development and the right to a healthy environment. These distinctions are partly political: Western governments have historically emphasised civil and political rights, while governments in the developing world have often argued that economic and social rights are equally important and should receive equal international attention.',
    'Human rights face several fundamental challenges. The first is the challenge of universality. Critics from postcolonial and non-Western traditions have argued that the current framework reflects particular Western liberal assumptions about the individual, the state, and the relationship between them — and that these assumptions do not necessarily resonate with all human cultures. The response of the human rights community has generally been to acknowledge that cultural variation exists while insisting that certain core protections — against torture, arbitrary execution, and slavery — are genuinely universal and cannot be relativised away.',
    'The second challenge is implementation. Even states that have ratified human rights conventions are frequently in violation of them. Enforcement mechanisms are weak: international human rights bodies can criticise, shame, and recommend — but they cannot compel compliance. Domestic courts can sometimes enforce human rights protections more effectively than international ones, particularly in countries with strong constitutional traditions. But in states where judicial independence is weak or where the executive controls the courts, domestic enforcement is equally unreliable.',
    'The third challenge is the relationship between rights and resources. Many of the most important human rights — the right to healthcare, to education, to an adequate standard of living — cannot be guaranteed without substantial financial investment. Poorer countries face a genuine dilemma: they may be willing to fulfil these rights but lack the resources to do so. This is one reason why the concept of "progressive realisation" was developed: the idea that states are not required to immediately achieve full fulfilment of economic and social rights, but are required to make demonstrable progress towards doing so, using the maximum of available resources.',
    'Human rights are not a finished project. The history of human rights is partly a history of expansion: groups that were once excluded from rights protection — women, people of colour, people with disabilities, LGBTQ+ individuals — have struggled for and won recognition. This expansion is ongoing. The question of which rights deserve recognition, and for whom, is not a settled one. It is a live political and moral debate in which citizens, activists, courts, and governments are all active participants.',
  ],
  dialogue: [],
  keyPhrases: [
    { phrase: 'inalienable rights', ru: 'неотчуждаемые права', context: 'Rights that cannot be permanently given up or taken away' },
    { phrase: 'progressive realisation', ru: 'прогрессивная реализация', context: 'The idea that economic/social rights must be fulfilled progressively, not instantly' },
    { phrase: 'universal declaration of human rights', ru: 'Всеобщая декларация прав человека', context: 'The 1948 UN document articulating 30 fundamental human rights' },
    { phrase: 'cultural relativism', ru: 'культурный релятивизм', context: 'The view that rights are culturally specific rather than universal' },
    { phrase: 'ratify', ru: 'ратифицировать', context: 'To officially approve and commit to a treaty or convention' },
  ],
  vocab: [
    { en: 'inalienable', ipa: '/ɪnˈeɪliənəbl/', pos: 'adjective', ru: 'неотчуждаемый', kz: 'бөлінбейтін', emoji: '🔒', definition: 'not able to be taken away from or given away by the person who possesses it', example: '"Human rights are inalienable — they cannot be legitimately removed by governments."', collocations: ['inalienable rights', 'inalienable dignity', 'inalienable freedom'] },
    { en: 'ratify', ipa: '/ˈrætɪfaɪ/', pos: 'verb', ru: 'ратифицировать', kz: 'бекіту', emoji: '✍️', definition: 'to give formal consent to a treaty or agreement, making it legally binding', example: '"Even states that have ratified human rights conventions are frequently in violation of them."', collocations: ['ratify a treaty', 'ratify a convention', 'refuse to ratify'] },
    { en: 'atrocity', ipa: '/əˈtrɒsɪti/', pos: 'noun', ru: 'злодеяние / зверство', kz: 'зұлымдық', emoji: '⚠️', definition: 'an extremely wicked or cruel act, typically one involving physical violence', example: '"The modern human rights system was created in response to the atrocities of World War Two."', collocations: ['commit an atrocity', 'wartime atrocity', 'respond to atrocities'] },
  ],
  prepTasks: [],
  task1Bank: [],
  task1Items: [
    { id: 't1', question: 'What does "universal" and "inalienable" mean in the context of human rights?', correct: 'Universal = applying to all people everywhere; inalienable = cannot be permanently surrendered or taken away' },
    { id: 't2', question: 'What event prompted the creation of the modern human rights regime?', correct: 'The Holocaust and other atrocities of World War Two' },
    { id: 't3', question: 'What is the difference between "first generation" and "second generation" rights?', correct: 'First generation = civil/political rights (speech, fair trial, vote); second generation = economic/social rights (work, education, healthcare)' },
    { id: 't4', question: 'What is the challenge of "universality" in human rights?', correct: 'Critics argue the framework reflects Western liberal assumptions that don\'t necessarily resonate with all cultures' },
    { id: 't5', question: 'What does "progressive realisation" mean?', correct: 'States are not required to immediately achieve full fulfilment of economic/social rights but must make demonstrable progress using maximum available resources' },
  ],
  task2Items: [
    { id: 's1', statement: 'Human rights are granted by governments and can be taken away by them.', correct: 'false' },
    { id: 's2', statement: 'The Universal Declaration of Human Rights was adopted in 1948.', correct: 'true' },
    { id: 's3', statement: 'International human rights bodies can compel states to comply with human rights standards.', correct: 'false' },
    { id: 's4', statement: 'The expansion of rights to previously excluded groups is described as an ongoing process.', correct: 'true' },
    { id: 's5', statement: 'The text says the human rights project is fully complete and settled.', correct: 'false' },
  ],
  listenChars: [], listenStatements: [],
  writingModel: [], writingTemplate: [], writingPhrases: [],
  grammarTable: [], grammarExtra: [],
  grammarTip: '',
  exercises: [],
  questions: [
    { id: 'q1', type: 'mc', cat: 'reading', text: 'What prompted the creation of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights?', opts: ['The end of the First World War', 'The formation of the United Nations', 'The Holocaust and atrocities of World War Two', 'The decolonisation movement'], correct: 'The Holocaust and atrocities of World War Two', hint: 'Para 2: "direct response to the Holocaust and the other atrocities of the war"', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q2', type: 'mc', cat: 'reading', text: 'Which is described as a "second generation" right?', opts: ['Freedom of speech', 'The right to vote', 'The right to education', 'Protection from torture'], correct: 'The right to education', hint: 'Para 3: "economic, social, and cultural rights — the right to… education"', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q3', type: 'mc', cat: 'reading', text: 'What is the "challenge of universality" in human rights?', opts: ['Rights apply to too many people', 'The framework reflects Western liberal assumptions not shared by all cultures', 'Different rights apply in different countries', 'Universal rights are too expensive to implement'], correct: 'The framework reflects Western liberal assumptions not shared by all cultures', hint: 'Para 4: "particular Western liberal assumptions"', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q4', type: 'mc', cat: 'reading', text: 'What does "progressive realisation" mean in the context of economic rights?', opts: ['Rights that are immediately and fully guaranteed', 'Rights that expand gradually to include new groups', 'Rights that must be worked towards progressively, using maximum available resources', 'Rights that are only relevant in developed countries'], correct: 'Rights that must be worked towards progressively, using maximum available resources', hint: 'Para 6: "make demonstrable progress towards doing so, using the maximum of available resources"', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q5', type: 'tf', cat: 'reading', text: 'International human rights bodies can compel states to comply with human rights standards.', opts: ['True', 'False'], correct: 'False', hint: 'Para 5: "can criticise, shame, and recommend — but they cannot compel compliance"', points: 1 },
    { id: 'q6', type: 'tf', cat: 'reading', text: 'Human rights are described as a finished project with settled membership.', opts: ['True', 'False'], correct: 'False', hint: 'Para 7: "human rights are not a finished project… ongoing"', points: 1 },
    { id: 'q7', type: 'mc', cat: 'grammar', text: '"Governments ___ not detain citizens without legal justification." (prohibition — cannot legally)', opts: ['should', 'may', 'must', 'ought to'], correct: 'must', hint: '"Must not" = prohibition — they are forbidden from doing this', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q8', type: 'mc', cat: 'grammar', text: '"Citizens ___ request legal aid if they cannot afford a lawyer." (= they have the right to)', opts: ['must', 'are entitled to', 'are required to', 'may'], correct: 'are entitled to', hint: '"Are entitled to" = have a legal right to', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q9', type: 'fill', cat: 'grammar', text: '"States ___ make demonstrable progress towards achieving economic rights." (obligation in law — are required)', correct: 'are required to', hint: '"Are required to + infinitive" = formal legal obligation', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q10', type: 'mc', cat: 'vocabulary', text: 'An "inalienable" right is one that…', opts: ['Can be temporarily suspended', 'Cannot be taken away or given up permanently', 'Only applies in democratic states', 'Is recognised in all domestic laws'], correct: 'Cannot be taken away or given up permanently', hint: 'Para 1: "cannot be permanently surrendered or transferred"', points: 1 },
    { id: 'q11', type: 'mc', cat: 'vocabulary', text: 'To "ratify" a convention means to…', opts: ['Criticise it publicly', 'Give formal consent, making it legally binding', 'Translate it into domestic law', 'Refuse to comply with it'], correct: 'Give formal consent, making it legally binding', hint: 'Para 5: "states that have ratified human rights conventions"', points: 1 },
    { id: 'q12', type: 'fill', cat: 'vocabulary', text: 'An extremely wicked or cruel act, typically involving violence, is called an ___.', correct: 'atrocity', hint: 'Para 2: "atrocities of the war"', points: 1 },
  ],
}

const L78_3: FullLesson = {
  id: 'l78-3', moduleNum: 20, lessonNum: 3,
  moduleTitle: 'Law, Rights & Justice',
  title: 'Justice Delayed: A Conversation About the Legal System',
  type: 'listening', xp: 80, duration: 50,
  paragraphs: [],
  dialogue: [
    { speaker: 'Priya', text: 'My cousin has been waiting three years for her case to go to trial. Three years. She was the victim of a serious crime and she still hasn\'t had a day in court.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Sasha', text: 'That\'s extremely common. The courts in most countries are severely backlogged. It\'s one of the biggest failures of modern justice systems — the principle that justice must be done, but also that it must be done in a timely way.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Priya', text: 'There\'s even a phrase for it — "justice delayed is justice denied." My cousin has had to live with this unresolved for three years. She\'s not allowed to move on properly. It affects her mental health, her work, everything.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Sasha', text: 'And you have to think about the accused as well. If someone is innocent, being under indictment for three years is essentially a punishment before any conviction. That\'s a fundamental problem.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Priya', text: 'I hadn\'t thought about it from that angle. What causes the delays? Is it just funding?', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Sasha', text: 'Funding is a big part of it — more judges, more court staff, more legal aid lawyers would reduce backlogs significantly. But there are also structural issues. Complex cases require extensive pre-trial processes: disclosure of evidence, expert witnesses, legal argument over admissibility. And both defence and prosecution are entitled to adequate time to prepare.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Priya', text: 'What about the accused\'s right to a fair trial? That should be protected, surely.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Sasha', text: 'Absolutely. The accused must be presumed innocent until proven guilty. They have the right to know the charges against them, to have access to the evidence, and to have a lawyer. In theory these rights are guaranteed. In practice, access to a good lawyer depends heavily on what you can afford, which undermines the whole idea of equal justice.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Priya', text: 'So if you\'re wealthy, you can hire a better lawyer and get a better outcome?', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Sasha', text: 'That\'s unfortunately very often the case. Research consistently shows that quality of legal representation correlates strongly with outcomes. Public defenders are typically much more overworked than private lawyers — they may have hundreds of clients at a time, which means they can\'t give adequate attention to each one.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Priya', text: 'That seems like a structural injustice built into the system.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Sasha', text: 'It is. And it\'s compounded by other inequalities — in how police treat different communities, in the discretion prosecutors have in deciding what to charge, in how juries are composed. The law on paper can be neutral; the law in practice rarely is.', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Priya', text: 'What would genuine reform look like?', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Sasha', text: 'More investment in legal aid. More use of alternative dispute resolution — mediation and arbitration — for cases that don\'t need to go to criminal court. Mandatory time limits on proceedings. Diversification of the judiciary. Reform of policing practices. And honestly — a political class that actually believes in a just legal system rather than just in winning elections by being "tough on crime."', isLeft: false },
    { speaker: 'Priya', text: 'That last one seems like the hardest to achieve.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Sasha', text: 'Without a doubt. But it\'s the one that makes all the others possible.', isLeft: false },
  ],
  keyPhrases: [
    { phrase: 'justice delayed is justice denied', ru: 'правосудие, отложенное в долгий ящик — это отказ в правосудии', context: 'If justice takes too long, it fails to be justice at all' },
    { phrase: 'presumed innocent', ru: 'считается невиновным', context: 'A legal principle: everyone is innocent until proven guilty' },
    { phrase: 'public defender', ru: 'государственный защитник', context: 'A lawyer paid by the state to represent defendants who cannot afford private counsel' },
    { phrase: 'alternative dispute resolution', ru: 'альтернативное урегулирование споров', context: 'Methods like mediation and arbitration that resolve conflicts outside court' },
    { phrase: 'under indictment', ru: 'под следствием / обвиняемый', context: 'Formally charged with a crime but not yet tried' },
  ],
  vocab: [
    { en: 'backlog', ipa: '/ˈbæklɒɡ/', pos: 'noun', ru: 'накопившиеся дела / задержка', kz: 'жинақталған жұмыс', emoji: '📋', definition: 'an accumulation of work or cases that have not yet been dealt with', example: '"Courts in most countries are severely backlogged — cases wait years for a hearing."', collocations: ['court backlog', 'clear the backlog', 'reduce the backlog'] },
    { en: 'indictment', ipa: '/ɪnˈdaɪtmənt/', pos: 'noun', ru: 'обвинительное заключение', kz: 'айыпталым', emoji: '📄', definition: 'a formal accusation that a person has committed a serious crime', example: '"Being under indictment for three years is essentially a punishment before conviction."', collocations: ['under indictment', 'face an indictment', 'issue an indictment'] },
    { en: 'admissibility', ipa: '/ədˌmɪsəˈbɪlɪti/', pos: 'noun', ru: 'допустимость (доказательств)', kz: 'жарамдылық', emoji: '✅', definition: 'the quality of evidence being acceptable for use in a court of law', example: '"Legal argument over the admissibility of evidence is one cause of pre-trial delay."', collocations: ['admissibility of evidence', 'rule on admissibility', 'challenge admissibility'] },
  ],
  prepTasks: [
    { id: 'p1', word: 'backlog', emoji: '📋', opts: ['an accumulation of undealt-with cases', 'a shortage of legal staff', 'a type of court procedure', 'an appeal against a conviction'], correct: 'an accumulation of undealt-with cases' },
    { id: 'p2', word: 'presumed innocent', emoji: '⚖️', opts: ['treated as not guilty until proven otherwise', 'released from custody automatically', 'assumed to be guilty until proven otherwise', 'given bail automatically'], correct: 'treated as not guilty until proven otherwise' },
  ],
  task1Bank: ['Priya', 'Sasha', 'Both', 'Neither'],
  task1Items: [
    { id: 't1', question: 'Whose cousin has been waiting three years for trial?', correct: 'Priya' },
    { id: 't2', question: 'Who explains that quality of legal representation correlates with outcomes?', correct: 'Sasha' },
    { id: 't3', question: 'Who describes the situation as a "structural injustice"?', correct: 'Priya' },
    { id: 't4', question: 'Who proposes investment in legal aid and mandatory time limits as reform measures?', correct: 'Sasha' },
    { id: 't5', question: 'Who agrees that the political class is the hardest obstacle to reform?', correct: 'Both' },
  ],
  task2Items: [
    { id: 's1', statement: 'Sasha says delays in justice only harm victims, not accused persons.', correct: 'false' },
    { id: 's2', statement: 'Public defenders typically have far fewer clients than private lawyers.', correct: 'false' },
    { id: 's3', statement: 'Sasha argues that the law on paper may be neutral, but the law in practice rarely is.', correct: 'true' },
    { id: 's4', statement: 'Sasha suggests that alternative dispute resolution could reduce court backlogs.', correct: 'true' },
    { id: 's5', statement: 'Priya had already considered the impact of delays on the accused before the conversation.', correct: 'false' },
  ],
  listenChars: [], listenStatements: [],
  writingModel: [], writingTemplate: [], writingPhrases: [],
  grammarTable: [], grammarExtra: [],
  grammarTip: '',
  exercises: [],
  questions: [
    { id: 'q1', type: 'mc', cat: 'listening', text: 'What effect has the three-year wait had on Priya\'s cousin?', opts: ['She has received a settlement from the court', 'It has affected her mental health, work, and ability to move on', 'She has had to pay large legal fees', 'She has had to move to another country'], correct: 'It has affected her mental health, work, and ability to move on', hint: 'Priya: "affects her mental health, her work, everything"', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q2', type: 'mc', cat: 'listening', text: 'What does Sasha say about being under indictment for three years?', opts: ['It is an appropriate part of the justice process', 'It is essentially a punishment before conviction for potentially innocent people', 'It helps defendants prepare their case properly', 'It is unavoidable in the current system'], correct: 'It is essentially a punishment before conviction for potentially innocent people', hint: 'Sasha: "essentially a punishment before any conviction"', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q3', type: 'mc', cat: 'listening', text: 'What does research show about the quality of legal representation?', opts: ['Quality of legal aid is equal to private representation', 'Quality of representation correlates strongly with legal outcomes', 'Public defenders win more cases than private lawyers', 'Better representation has no effect on outcomes'], correct: 'Quality of representation correlates strongly with legal outcomes', hint: 'Sasha: "quality of legal representation correlates strongly with outcomes"', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q4', type: 'mc', cat: 'listening', text: 'Which reform does Sasha NOT suggest?', opts: ['More investment in legal aid', 'Abolishing all criminal courts', 'Mandatory time limits on proceedings', 'Alternative dispute resolution'], correct: 'Abolishing all criminal courts', hint: 'Sasha lists specific reforms — abolishing courts is not among them', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q5', type: 'tf', cat: 'listening', text: 'Sasha argues that inequalities in the justice system are only related to funding.', opts: ['True', 'False'], correct: 'False', hint: 'Sasha: also mentions policing, prosecutorial discretion, jury composition', points: 1 },
    { id: 'q6', type: 'tf', cat: 'listening', text: 'Sasha says political will for genuine reform is the hardest thing to achieve.', opts: ['True', 'False'], correct: 'True', hint: 'Sasha: "Without a doubt. But it\'s the one that makes all the others possible."', points: 1 },
    { id: 'q7', type: 'mc', cat: 'grammar', text: '"The accused ___ be presumed innocent until proven guilty." (legal principle — must)', opts: ['should', 'must', 'may', 'would'], correct: 'must', hint: '"Must be" = strong legal obligation/requirement', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q8', type: 'fill', cat: 'grammar', text: '"Both prosecution and defence ___ (= have the right to) adequate time to prepare their case."', correct: 'are entitled to', hint: '"Are entitled to + noun" = have a legal right to', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q9', type: 'mc', cat: 'grammar', text: '"Judges ___ disclose any personal interest in a case — it is prohibited." (must not)', opts: ['must not', 'should not', 'need not', 'would not'], correct: 'must not', hint: '"Must not" = prohibition in legal/professional context', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q10', type: 'mc', cat: 'vocabulary', text: 'A "court backlog" means there are…', opts: ['Too many judges for the number of cases', 'Cases waiting to be heard that have accumulated over time', 'An appeal process blocking new cases', 'More lawyers than are needed'], correct: 'Cases waiting to be heard that have accumulated over time', hint: 'Sasha: "courts are severely backlogged"', points: 1 },
    { id: 'q11', type: 'mc', cat: 'vocabulary', text: 'Being "under indictment" means…', opts: ['Having been convicted and sentenced', 'Formally accused but not yet tried', 'Having been released on bail', 'Being investigated but not yet charged'], correct: 'Formally accused but not yet tried', hint: 'Sasha: "being under indictment for three years"', points: 1 },
    { id: 'q12', type: 'fill', cat: 'vocabulary', text: '"Alternative ___ resolution" refers to methods like mediation and arbitration outside court.', correct: 'dispute', hint: 'Sasha: "alternative dispute resolution"', points: 1 },
  ],
}

const L78_3B: FullLesson = {
  id: 'l78-3b', moduleNum: 20, lessonNum: 4,
  moduleTitle: 'Law, Rights & Justice',
  title: 'Restorative Justice: Beyond Punishment to Healing',
  type: 'listening', xp: 80, duration: 50,
  paragraphs: [],
  dialogue: [
    { speaker: 'Dr. Mwangi', text: 'Good afternoon. I want to introduce you to a model of justice that challenges many of our deepest assumptions about what justice is for — restorative justice. In the dominant model of criminal justice, crime is understood as an offence against the state. The state prosecutes; the defendant either admits or denies; a verdict is reached; a sentence is imposed. The victim is, in many ways, peripheral to this process. They are a witness, perhaps, but not a party. Justice is something done by and for the state, not by and for the people most directly affected.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Dr. Mwangi', text: 'Restorative justice offers a fundamentally different framework. In restorative justice, crime is understood as a rupture in a relationship — between an offender and a victim, and often between an offender and a broader community. The aim of justice is not primarily to punish the offender but to repair the harm caused. This is achieved through a structured process of dialogue in which victims can articulate what has been done to them and what they need to move forward, offenders can acknowledge responsibility and understand the impact of their actions, and communities can be part of identifying meaningful responses.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Dr. Mwangi', text: 'The evidence for restorative justice is, in many respects, impressive. A growing body of research — including multiple randomised controlled trials and systematic reviews — suggests that restorative justice processes lead to higher levels of victim satisfaction, higher rates of offender compliance with agreed reparations, and significantly lower rates of reoffending compared to conventional criminal justice processes. These are not trivial findings. In a justice system that is supposed to prevent crime and support victims, restorative justice often outperforms the conventional model on both counts.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Dr. Mwangi', text: 'I want to address some common objections. First: is restorative justice "soft on crime"? I would argue it is precisely the opposite. Facing the person you have harmed, hearing directly from them about the impact of your actions, and being held accountable by your community requires more moral courage than most prison sentences. Prison allows many offenders to avoid genuine accountability — they serve their time and are released, having never confronted the reality of their actions. Restorative justice cannot be avoided in the same way.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Dr. Mwangi', text: 'Second: does restorative justice work for serious crimes — murder, sexual violence, serious assaults? This is a genuinely difficult question. Restorative processes in serious cases require careful facilitation, the voluntary participation of the victim, and significant support. They cannot be mandated — participation must be entirely voluntary. But the evidence suggests that even in the most serious cases, restorative dialogue can be profoundly healing for victims when it is done well and when victims choose it freely.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Dr. Mwangi', text: 'Third: does restorative justice ignore structural injustice? A legitimate concern. If we focus only on individual relationships, we may miss the social conditions — poverty, inequality, racism — that produce crime in the first place. Restorative justice proponents acknowledge this limitation. Restorative justice is not a replacement for structural reform; it is a complement to it. We need both.', isLeft: true },
    { speaker: 'Dr. Mwangi', text: 'I want to leave you with a question: what do we actually want from a justice system? If our primary aim is revenge or deterrence through punishment, then the conventional model has its logic. If our primary aim is healing — for victims, for offenders, for communities — then restorative justice offers a compelling alternative. I do not think these two aims are entirely incompatible. But I do think that genuinely prioritising healing requires a fundamental rethinking of what justice means.', isLeft: true },
  ],
  keyPhrases: [
    { phrase: 'restorative justice', ru: 'восстановительное правосудие', context: 'A model of justice focused on repairing harm and relationships rather than punishment' },
    { phrase: 'offender', ru: 'правонарушитель', context: 'A person who has committed a crime or offence' },
    { phrase: 'reoffending', ru: 'рецидивизм / повторное преступление', context: 'Committing another crime after having already been convicted' },
    { phrase: 'reparations', ru: 'возмещение ущерба', context: 'Compensation or amends made to those who have been harmed' },
    { phrase: 'deterrence', ru: 'сдерживание / устрашение', context: 'The use of punishment to discourage others from committing crimes' },
  ],
  vocab: [
    { en: 'restorative justice', ipa: '/rɪˈstɔːrətɪv ˈdʒʌstɪs/', pos: 'noun phrase', ru: 'восстановительное правосудие', kz: 'қалпына келтіруші əділет', emoji: '🤝', definition: 'an approach to justice focused on repairing harm to victims and communities rather than punishing offenders', example: '"Restorative justice leads to higher victim satisfaction and lower reoffending rates."', collocations: ['practice restorative justice', 'restorative justice programme', 'restorative justice approach'] },
    { en: 'reoffending', ipa: '/ˌriːəˈfendɪŋ/', pos: 'noun', ru: 'рецидивизм / повторные преступления', kz: 'рецидив', emoji: '🔄', definition: 'committing crimes again after having already been convicted and punished', example: '"Restorative justice processes lead to significantly lower rates of reoffending."', collocations: ['reduce reoffending', 'high rate of reoffending', 'prevent reoffending'] },
    { en: 'reparation', ipa: '/ˌrepəˈreɪʃən/', pos: 'noun', ru: 'возмещение ущерба / репарации', kz: 'өтем', emoji: '🔧', definition: 'the act of making amends for wrongdoing; compensation for harm caused', example: '"Restorative justice involves offenders making agreed reparations to those they harmed."', collocations: ['make reparations', 'offer reparations', 'agreed reparations'] },
  ],
  prepTasks: [
    { id: 'p1', word: 'restorative justice', emoji: '🤝', opts: ['justice focused on repairing harm rather than punishment', 'a legal appeal system', 'a type of prison programme', 'mediation between lawyers only'], correct: 'justice focused on repairing harm rather than punishment' },
    { id: 'p2', word: 'reoffending', emoji: '🔄', opts: ['committing crimes again after conviction', 'being convicted of the same crime twice', 'refusing to comply with a sentence', 'failing to attend court'], correct: 'committing crimes again after conviction' },
  ],
  task1Bank: [],
  task1Items: [
    { id: 't1', question: 'How does conventional criminal justice understand crime, versus restorative justice?', correct: 'Conventional: crime as offence against the state; Restorative: crime as a rupture in a relationship, to be repaired' },
    { id: 't2', question: 'What does evidence show about restorative justice outcomes?', correct: 'Higher victim satisfaction, higher compliance with reparations, significantly lower reoffending' },
    { id: 't3', question: 'How does Dr. Mwangi respond to the accusation that restorative justice is "soft on crime"?', correct: 'It is the opposite — facing your victim and community requires more moral courage than serving a prison sentence, which allows avoidance' },
    { id: 't4', question: 'Under what condition can restorative justice work even in serious cases?', correct: 'When participation is entirely voluntary, with careful facilitation and significant support for the victim' },
    { id: 't5', question: 'What does Dr. Mwangi say about restorative justice and structural injustice?', correct: 'Restorative justice acknowledges this limitation — it is a complement to structural reform, not a replacement' },
  ],
  task2Items: [
    { id: 's1', statement: 'In the conventional justice model, the victim is the central party.', correct: 'false' },
    { id: 's2', statement: 'Dr. Mwangi says restorative justice consistently outperforms conventional justice at reducing reoffending.', correct: 'true' },
    { id: 's3', statement: 'Victims must be required to participate in restorative justice processes.', correct: 'false' },
    { id: 's4', statement: 'Dr. Mwangi says restorative justice and structural reform are mutually exclusive.', correct: 'false' },
    { id: 's5', statement: 'Dr. Mwangi argues that healing and punishment as justice aims are entirely incompatible.', correct: 'false' },
  ],
  listenChars: [], listenStatements: [],
  writingModel: [], writingTemplate: [], writingPhrases: [],
  grammarTable: [], grammarExtra: [],
  grammarTip: '',
  exercises: [],
  questions: [
    { id: 'q1', type: 'mc', cat: 'listening', text: 'In the conventional criminal justice model, who is justice primarily done "by and for"?', opts: ['The victim', 'The offender', 'The state', 'The community'], correct: 'The state', hint: 'Lecture: "justice is something done by and for the state"', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q2', type: 'mc', cat: 'listening', text: 'What does research show about reoffending rates in restorative justice?', opts: ['They are higher than in conventional justice', 'They are the same as in conventional justice', 'They are significantly lower than in conventional justice', 'Results are inconclusive'], correct: 'They are significantly lower than in conventional justice', hint: 'Lecture: "significantly lower rates of reoffending compared to conventional criminal justice"', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q3', type: 'mc', cat: 'listening', text: 'How does Dr. Mwangi argue restorative justice is NOT soft on crime?', opts: ['It imposes longer prison sentences', 'Facing your victim requires more moral courage than prison', 'It fines offenders more heavily', 'It publishes offenders\' names in the media'], correct: 'Facing your victim requires more moral courage than prison', hint: 'Lecture: "requires more moral courage than most prison sentences"', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q4', type: 'mc', cat: 'listening', text: 'What condition must be met for restorative justice in serious cases?', opts: ['The offender must accept a prison sentence first', 'Participation must be entirely voluntary for the victim', 'A judge must be present throughout', 'The case must have been through the conventional courts first'], correct: 'Participation must be entirely voluntary for the victim', hint: 'Lecture: "participation must be entirely voluntary"', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q5', type: 'tf', cat: 'listening', text: 'Dr. Mwangi argues that restorative justice should replace structural reform efforts.', opts: ['True', 'False'], correct: 'False', hint: 'Lecture: "not a replacement… a complement to it. We need both."', points: 1 },
    { id: 'q6', type: 'tf', cat: 'listening', text: 'Dr. Mwangi says that revenge/deterrence and healing are completely incompatible justice aims.', opts: ['True', 'False'], correct: 'False', hint: 'Lecture: "I do not think these two aims are entirely incompatible"', points: 1 },
    { id: 'q7', type: 'mc', cat: 'grammar', text: '"Victims ___ not be required to participate — it must be entirely voluntary." (prohibition)', opts: ['should', 'must', 'may', 'will'], correct: 'must', hint: '"Must not be required" = it is forbidden to require participation', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q8', type: 'fill', cat: 'grammar', text: '"Offenders ___ (= are required to) acknowledge the full impact of their actions on victims." (legal obligation)', correct: 'are required to', hint: '"Are required to + infinitive" = formal obligation', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q9', type: 'mc', cat: 'grammar', text: '"Victims ___ choose whichever form of justice process meets their needs." (= have the right to)', opts: ['must', 'need to', 'are entitled to', 'are required to'], correct: 'are entitled to', hint: '"Are entitled to + verb" = have the right to choose', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q10', type: 'mc', cat: 'vocabulary', text: '"Reoffending" means…', opts: ['Being tried twice for the same crime', 'Committing further crimes after conviction', 'Refusing to accept a sentence', 'Appealing a court decision'], correct: 'Committing further crimes after conviction', hint: 'Lecture: "lower rates of reoffending"', points: 1 },
    { id: 'q11', type: 'mc', cat: 'vocabulary', text: '"Reparations" in a restorative justice context are…', opts: ['Prison sentences', 'Compensation or amends made to those who were harmed', 'Legal fees paid to the court', 'Community service hours'], correct: 'Compensation or amends made to those who were harmed', hint: 'Lecture: "offender compliance with agreed reparations"', points: 1 },
    { id: 'q12', type: 'fill', cat: 'vocabulary', text: 'Using punishment to discourage people from committing crimes is called ___.', correct: 'deterrence', hint: 'Lecture: "deterrence through punishment"', points: 1 },
  ],
}

const L78_G1: FullLesson = {
  id: 'l78-g1', moduleNum: 20, lessonNum: 5,
  moduleTitle: 'Law, Rights & Justice',
  title: 'Grammar: Modal Verbs for Obligation, Permission, and Prohibition',
  type: 'grammar', xp: 90, duration: 60,
  paragraphs: [],
  dialogue: [],
  keyPhrases: [],
  vocab: [],
  prepTasks: [],
  task1Bank: [], task1Items: [], task2Items: [],
  listenChars: [], listenStatements: [],
  writingModel: [], writingTemplate: [], writingPhrases: [],
  grammarTip: 'Модальные глаголы в юридическом и формальном письме:\n\nОБЯЗАТЕЛЬСТВО / ТРЕБОВАНИЕ:\nshall → юридически обязателен: "The tenant shall pay rent by the 1st."\nmust → строгое требование: "All documents must be submitted."\n\nРАЗРЕШЕНИЕ:\nmay → официальное разрешение: "The committee may adjourn the meeting."\ncan → менее формально: "You can request an extension."\n\nРЕКОМЕНДАЦИЯ:\nshould → рекомендуется: "Applicants should submit all required documents."\nought to → менее частый формальный вариант\n\n⚠️ Юридическое "shall" ≠ обычное "will":\n"I shall be there." (формальное обещание)\n"All parties shall comply." (юридическое обязательство)',
  grammarTable: [
    { subject: 'must',          verb: 'strong obligation / prohibition',  short: 'it is required / it is forbidden', example: '"All defendants must be informed of the charges against them." / "Courts must not be politically influenced."' },
    { subject: 'have to / need to', verb: 'external necessity',         short: 'it is necessary (external rule)', example: '"Witnesses have to tell the truth under oath." / "Lawyers need to register with the bar."' },
    { subject: 'should / ought to', verb: 'recommendation / moral duty', short: 'it is advisable / morally right', example: '"Judges should declare conflicts of interest." / "Citizens ought to be aware of their rights."' },
    { subject: 'may',           verb: 'formal permission / prohibition', short: 'is permitted / is not permitted', example: '"The accused may remain silent." / "Evidence may not be withheld from the defence."' },
    { subject: 'can / cannot',  verb: 'ability-based permission / prohibition', short: 'is able to / is not able to (legally)', example: '"Anyone can access the court records." / "You cannot be convicted without evidence."' },
    { subject: 'be allowed to / be permitted to', verb: 'passive permission', short: 'is granted permission', example: '"Defendants are allowed to consult a lawyer." / "Juveniles are not permitted to appear in adult courts."' },
  ],
  grammarExtra: [
    {
      title: 'Must vs Have To: Strong vs External Obligation',
      color: N, bg: '#EEF2F7', border: 'rgba(0,56,118,0.15)',
      rows: [
        { form: 'Must = internal/strong obligation (often moral or legal principle)', example: '"Governments must protect their citizens\' rights." (a moral/legal imperative)' },
        { form: 'Have to = external obligation (imposed by rules or authority)', example: '"Lawyers have to pay annual registration fees." (an external rule)' },
        { form: 'Must not = strong prohibition (a legal or moral ban)', example: '"Judges must not accept gifts from parties in a case." (categorically forbidden)' },
        { form: 'Don\'t have to = no obligation (but it is permitted)', example: '"Suspects don\'t have to answer police questions." (they have the right to remain silent)' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'May, Can, Be Allowed To: Permission in Formal Contexts',
      color: T, bg: '#E6F7F2', border: 'rgba(29,158,117,0.2)',
      rows: [
        { form: 'May (formal permission)', example: '"Any citizen may request access to public court records."' },
        { form: 'May not (formal prohibition)', example: '"The jury may not communicate with either party during the trial."' },
        { form: 'Be allowed to (less formal permission)', example: '"Defendants are allowed to have a lawyer present during questioning."' },
        { form: 'Cannot (strong prohibition — not possible legally)', example: '"A person cannot be tried twice for the same crime." (legal principle)' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Should and Ought To: Moral and Professional Obligation',
      color: G, bg: '#FEF6E8', border: 'rgba(201,147,59,0.2)',
      rows: [
        { form: 'Should = recommendation or expected behaviour', example: '"Witnesses should answer questions truthfully and completely."' },
        { form: 'Ought to = moral duty (slightly more formal than should)', example: '"Prosecutors ought to disclose all relevant evidence to the defence."' },
        { form: 'Should have + pp = past obligation not fulfilled (regret/criticism)', example: '"The judge should have recused himself — he had a conflict of interest."' },
        { form: 'Ought to have + pp = same as should have (slightly more formal)', example: '"The evidence ought to have been disclosed earlier."' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Formal Legal Language: Shall, Be Required To, Be Obliged To',
      color: S, bg: '#E8F4FB', border: 'rgba(27,143,196,0.2)',
      rows: [
        { form: 'Shall (in legal documents) = mandatory obligation (formal written law)', example: '"All parties shall be notified of the hearing date." (in a statute or contract)' },
        { form: 'Be required to + infinitive = external obligation', example: '"Employers are required to provide written contracts." / "States are required to provide legal aid."' },
        { form: 'Be obliged to + infinitive = obligated to do', example: '"Lawyers are obliged to maintain client confidentiality." (professional duty)' },
        { form: 'Be prohibited from + gerund = cannot do this', example: '"Police are prohibited from questioning suspects without a lawyer present."' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Common Errors with Modal Verbs in Legal Contexts',
      color: '#DC2626', bg: '#FEF2F2', border: '#FECACA',
      rows: [
        { form: '❌ Suspects must not answer questions. (= they are forbidden)', example: '✅ Suspects don\'t have to answer questions. (= they have the right to remain silent, but may speak if they wish)' },
        { form: '❌ The judge should recuse himself last week.', example: '✅ The judge should have recused himself last week. (past unfulfilled obligation = should + have + pp)' },
        { form: '❌ Lawyers are allowed from revealing client information.', example: '✅ Lawyers are prohibited from revealing client information. (prohibited from + gerund)' },
        { form: '❌ All citizens can to vote.', example: '✅ All citizens can vote. / All citizens are allowed to vote. (no "to" after "can")' },
      ],
    },
  ],
  exercises: [
    { id: 'e1', type: 'mc', instruction: 'Choose the correct modal.', text: '"The accused ___ be presumed innocent until proven guilty." (strong legal principle)', opts: ['should', 'must', 'may', 'can'], correct: 'must', hint: '"Must be" = strong legal requirement/principle' },
    { id: 'e2', type: 'mc', instruction: 'Choose the correct modal for formal permission.', text: '"The defendant ___ remain silent when questioned by police."', opts: ['must', 'should', 'may', 'has to'], correct: 'may', hint: '"May" = formal permission (the right to)' },
    { id: 'e3', type: 'fill', instruction: 'Complete with the correct modal.', text: '"Police ___ (= are forbidden from) question a suspect without a lawyer present." (prohibition)', correct: 'are prohibited from', hint: '"Are prohibited from + gerund" = formal prohibition' },
    { id: 'e4', type: 'mc', instruction: 'Choose the correct modal for no obligation.', text: '"Suspects ___ answer police questions — they have the right to remain silent."', opts: ['must not', 'don\'t have to', 'may not', 'cannot'], correct: 'don\'t have to', hint: '"Don\'t have to" = no obligation (but they may if they choose)' },
    { id: 'e5', type: 'fill', instruction: 'Complete with the correct form.', text: '"Lawyers ___ (= are obligated to) maintain client confidentiality at all times." (professional duty)', correct: 'are obliged to / are required to', hint: '"Are obliged/required to + infinitive" = professional/legal obligation' },
    { id: 'e6', type: 'mc', instruction: 'Choose the correct modal for past unfulfilled obligation.', text: '"The judge ___ recused himself — he had a clear conflict of interest."', opts: ['must recuse', 'should have recused', 'had to recuse', 'must have recused'], correct: 'should have recused', hint: '"Should have + pp" = past obligation not fulfilled (criticism/regret)' },
    { id: 'e7', type: 'fill', instruction: 'Complete with a formal legal modal.', text: '"All parties ___ (= are required in law) submit written evidence before the hearing date." (legal document style)', correct: 'shall', hint: '"Shall" in legal documents = mandatory obligation (written law)' },
    { id: 'e8', type: 'mc', instruction: 'Choose the correct modal for strong prohibition.', text: '"Jurors ___ discuss the case with anyone outside the court during the trial."', opts: ['don\'t have to', 'should not', 'must not', 'may not prefer to'], correct: 'must not', hint: '"Must not" = strong prohibition — it is categorically forbidden' },
    { id: 'e9', type: 'fill', instruction: 'Complete with the correct expression.', text: '"Citizens ___ (= have the right to) vote in all public elections once they reach legal age."', correct: 'are entitled to / are allowed to', hint: '"Are entitled to / allowed to + infinitive" = have the right to' },
    { id: 'e10', type: 'mc', instruction: 'Which expresses a recommendation, not a strong obligation?', text: '', opts: ['"Lawyers must disclose conflicts of interest."', '"Courts must not interfere with jury deliberations."', '"Prosecutors should disclose all relevant evidence."', '"States shall provide legal aid to those who cannot afford it."'], correct: '"Prosecutors should disclose all relevant evidence."', hint: '"Should" = recommendation/professional expectation — softer than "must"' },
    { id: 'e11', type: 'fill', instruction: 'Choose the correct form.', text: '"Evidence ___ (= is forbidden from being) withheld from the defence." (legal prohibition — passive)', correct: 'may not be / must not be', hint: '"May not be withheld" or "must not be withheld" = formal passive prohibition' },
    { id: 'e12', type: 'mc', instruction: 'Choose the correct form.', text: '"The witness ___ lie under oath — perjury is a serious criminal offence."', opts: ['should not', 'don\'t have to', 'must not', 'may not prefer to'], correct: 'must not', hint: '"Must not" = strong prohibition', points: 1 },
    { id: 'e13', type: 'fill', instruction: 'Rewrite using "be prohibited from."', text: '"Lawyers cannot reveal their clients\' communications to the police." → Lawyers ___ revealing their clients\' communications to the police.', correct: 'are prohibited from', hint: '"Are prohibited from + gerund" = formal prohibition' },
    { id: 'e14', type: 'mc', instruction: 'Choose the correct modal for a past obligation that was not fulfilled.', text: '"The evidence ___ submitted earlier — the delay caused serious harm to the claimant."', opts: ['must have been submitted', 'should have been submitted', 'had to be submitted', 'was supposed to submit'], correct: 'should have been submitted', hint: '"Should have been + pp" = past obligation not fulfilled (passive)', points: 1 },
    { id: 'e15', type: 'fill', instruction: 'Complete using "shall" (formal legal document style).', text: '"The court ___ notify both parties of the hearing date at least 14 days in advance."', correct: 'shall', hint: '"Shall" in legal documents = mandatory obligation', points: 1 },
    { id: 'e16', type: 'mc', instruction: 'Identify the error.', text: '"Defendants are allowed from speaking to their lawyer before questioning."', opts: ['No error', '"From" should be "to"', '"Questioning" should be "question"', '"Are allowed" should be "must"'], correct: '"From" should be "to"', hint: '"Are allowed TO + infinitive" (not "from"); "prohibited FROM + gerund"' },
  ],
  questions: [
    { id: 'q1', type: 'mc', cat: 'grammar', text: '"All suspects ___ be treated equally under the law — regardless of social status." (legal principle)', opts: ['should', 'must', 'may', 'ought to'], correct: 'must', hint: '"Must" = strong legal obligation/principle', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q2', type: 'fill', cat: 'grammar', text: '"The accused ___ (= has the legal right to) know the specific charges against them."', correct: 'is entitled to / is allowed to', hint: '"Is entitled to + infinitive" = has the legal right to', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q3', type: 'mc', cat: 'grammar', text: '"Suspects ___ answer questions — they have the right to remain silent." (no obligation)', opts: ['must not', 'cannot', 'don\'t have to', 'may not'], correct: 'don\'t have to', hint: '"Don\'t have to" = no obligation, they may speak if they wish', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q4', type: 'mc', cat: 'grammar', text: '"The judge ___ accepted gifts from either party — it is a fundamental breach of judicial ethics."', opts: ['must not', 'should not have', 'should not', 'must not have'], correct: 'should not have', hint: '"Should not have + pp" = past action that was done but shouldn\'t have been', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q5', type: 'fill', cat: 'grammar', text: '"Police ___ (= are formally forbidden from) using excessive force during arrest." (formal passive prohibition)', correct: 'are prohibited from using / must not use', hint: '"Are prohibited from + gerund" or "must not use" = formal prohibition', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q6', type: 'tf', cat: 'grammar', text: '"Must not" and "don\'t have to" have the same meaning in legal English.', opts: ['True', 'False'], correct: 'False', hint: '"Must not" = forbidden; "don\'t have to" = no obligation but permitted', points: 1 },
    { id: 'q7', type: 'fill', cat: 'grammar', text: '"In the contract: All parties ___ submit claims within 30 days of the incident." (legal document style)', correct: 'shall', hint: '"Shall" in legal documents = formal mandatory obligation', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q8', type: 'mc', cat: 'grammar', text: '"Evidence ___ not be withheld from the defence." (formal legal prohibition — passive)', opts: ['should', 'may', 'can', 'will'], correct: 'may', hint: '"May not be withheld" = formal legal prohibition (passive)', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q9', type: 'fill', cat: 'grammar', text: '"Lawyers ___ (= are legally required to) report suspicions of money laundering to the authorities."', correct: 'are required to / are obliged to', hint: '"Are required to / obliged to + infinitive" = legal/professional obligation', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q10', type: 'mc', cat: 'grammar', text: '"Prosecutors ___ disclosed the evidence earlier — the late disclosure was a serious procedural error."', opts: ['must have', 'should have', 'had to have', 'ought to'], correct: 'should have', hint: '"Should have + pp" = past obligation not fulfilled', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q11', type: 'fill', cat: 'grammar', text: '"Citizens ___ (= have the right to) appeal against decisions made by lower courts."', correct: 'are entitled to / are allowed to', hint: '"Are entitled to" = have the legal right to', points: 2 },
    { id: 'q12', type: 'mc', cat: 'grammar', text: 'Which sentence expresses a recommendation, not a legal requirement?', opts: ['"Lawyers must maintain client confidentiality."', '"Witnesses must not lie under oath."', '"Judges should declare any conflicts of interest."', '"States shall provide legal aid."'], correct: '"Judges should declare any conflicts of interest."', hint: '"Should" = professional expectation/recommendation — softer than "must"', points: 2 },
  ],
}

